{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgRykiw3bg4q48shWCZAn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed three separate problems and wrote them up in `ANSWER.md`. The full flow now works locally against a stand-in for Intervals. The scope fix (cause 2) hasn't been tried against the real Intervals.icu yet.\n\n1. **The Connect button always got \"Unauthorized\".** The app proves who you're signed in as with a header that only its own data requests add. The button was a plain link, so clicking it opened a page without that header and the server rejected every athlete. Now the button asks the server for the Intervals sign-in link in the background, with the header, and then opens that link. Changed in `static/app.js` and `auth_init` in `server.py`.\n\n2. **Intervals refused the request before showing the consent screen.** Intervals expects one permission per area, and \"write\" access already includes \"read\". When calendar sync shipped on 2026-10-02, the list became `CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`, and that's when the `invalid_request` errors started. Rivera's successful connection backs this up: we asked for activity read and write, and Intervals granted just `ACTIVITY:WRITE`. The list is now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE` in `config.py`, which still covers everything the app uses.\n   - This is worked out from the logs and the documented permission format. Intervals' error doesn't say which part it objects to.\n   - If it still fails, check next that the callback address in the config exactly matches the one registered with Intervals.\n\n3. **Working connections were marked \"expired, reconnect\" after an hour.** Intervals sign-in tokens never expire, so its response has no expiry time and no renewal token. The code filled the missing expiry with a made-up one hour, then threw away a valid token when the hour was up. Now a token with no expiry date is kept indefinitely. A connection is only marked expired when Intervals actually rejects the token, which means the athlete revoked access.\n\n**Testing:** I ran the server against a stand-in Intervals server I wrote, kept outside the repo. Without the sign-in header the server still refuses. With it, it returns the Intervals link with the new permission list. The callback finishes the connection, and status shows the athlete connected with a token that doesn't expire.\n\nRivera's saved token was already wiped, so they need to connect once more; the button now works for that. Nothing is committed yet."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1532,"cache_read_input_tokens":33126,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1532},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0406ac1d-fd2f-4273-a278-2be16b18cfff","uuid":"81e7b1fa-7e68-4dba-bfe9-dfe4c1e41c83","timestamp":"2026-10-27T02:48:35.388Z","request_id":"req_011CgRykifBED3nF5hT1CRzA"}
